From 41401c19441568c28efeea5a8f6007600d2ff830 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dev <dev@perseus>
Date: Sat, 25 Jul 2026 11:30:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 06/12] NATIVEINT=32: fix bootstrap DFT encoding, composite
 scaling factor lookup, sparse-encapsulated composite raise

Three fixes that together make CKKS bootstrapping work at NATIVEINT=32, via composite
scaling. All three were validated on the CPU alone, against the same symptom the GPU
shows without them: all-NaN output, logstd=inf.

1) FHECKKSRNS::FitToNativeVector (int64 overload) had no NATIVEINT == 32 branch.

   The exact twin of the CKKSPackedEncoding::FitToNativeVector defect already fixed by this
   patch series, and it was missed. MakeAuxPlaintext hands it int64_t coefficients in
   [0, Max64BitValue()) with negatives stored as bigBound + x; with NativeInteger = uint32_t,
   NativeInteger(vec[i]), bigValueHf = bigBound >> 1 and diff = bigBound - modulus all
   truncate, so every coefficient gets a garbage CRT residue. This overload encodes EVERY
   bootstrap DFT plaintext (the CoeffsToSlots / SlotsToCoeffs matrices), so the homomorphic
   DFT was garbage even though the encoder-side twin was already correct.

   Measured effect: bootstrap output goes from all-NaN (2048/2048 non-finite) to finite --
   matching what a NATIVEINT=64 build produces on the same chain. The residual error is then
   a parameter-regime limit, not a bug (below), so this alone does not fix the 27-bit chain.

2) MakeAuxPlaintext's mixed-size-chain scale adjustment (from the mixedlimb patch) breaks
   COMPOSITESCALING*.

   COMPOSITESCALING* deliberately stores a SENTINEL 1 in m_scalingFactorsReal at every level
   index that is not a multiple of compositeDegree (ckksrns-cryptoparameters.cpp, the
   "else m_scalingFactorsReal[k] = 1" arm). The boundary formula
       boundary = sf[level+1] * qDrop / sf[level]
   reads that sentinel and collapses scFact to qDrop/sf[level] -- measured 2^-27 on a
   2x27-bit composite chain -- killing bootstrap setup with "Scaling factor too small".
   Composite chains are uniform by construction, so the mixed-size adjustment simply does
   not apply; skip it there. Uniform and mixed FLEXIBLE* chains are bit-identical to before.

3) SPARSE_ENCAPSULATED + COMPOSITESCALING: supported instead of rejected.

   Upstream rejects the combination in gen-cryptocontext-params-validation.cpp. The blocker
   is that KeySwitchGenSparse/KeySwitchSparse are a single-tower (q0, p) fast path: the
   composite bottom level has compositeDegree towers whose integer representative lives mod
   q0*...*q_{d-1}, so the per-tower SwitchModulus extension is not even well-defined there
   -- and KeySwitchSparse returns a 1-tower ciphertext that the composite ExtendCiphertext
   then over-reads (GetElementAtIndex(j), j < d) => segfault.

   Fix: on composite chains generate the M-4 encapsulation key with the standard
   algo->KeySwitchGen(dense -> sparse) and apply both directions with the standard hybrid
   keyswitch around ExtendCiphertext -- exactly how the M-2 (sparse -> dense) direction has
   always worked. The single-prime KeySwitchSparse fast path is untouched for d == 1.

WHY THIS MATTERS (the parameter wall): CKKS bootstrapping needs a scaling factor of roughly
42+ bits. Measured on a NATIVEINT=64 build, sweeping prime size with everything else fixed
(logN=12, budget 4:3, SPARSE_ENCAPSULATED):

    scale bits   27      30     34     38     42      46       50       53
    err_max      13685   51.6   3.02   15.6   0.022   7.7e-4   8.8e-5   9.3e-6

NATIVEINT=32 caps primes at 28 bits (MAX_MODULUS_SIZE), so a single-prime-per-level 32-bit
chain CANNOT bootstrap -- arithmetic, not a code defect (a 64-bit build fails identically on
a 27-bit chain; correction factor 1..13, secret dist, scaling technique, level budget, depth
and big-q0/small-delta were all swept and eliminated). COMPOSITESCALING is the way out, and
is exactly what (2) and (3) unblock. Validated results, NATIVEINT=32 build, logN=12,
COMPOSITESCALINGMANUAL compositeDegree=2, 2x27-bit primes (effective 54-bit scale),
budget 4:3, encode level = sizeQ - 2*d:

    UNIFORM_TERNARY       -> 0/2048 non-finite, err_max 5.6e-4  = 10.8 bits
    SPARSE_ENCAPSULATED   -> 0/2048 non-finite, err_max 1.6e-6  = 19.2 bits

The sparse-encapsulated composite bootstrap EXCEEDS the NATIVEINT=64 single-prime baseline
at the same ring size (17.8 bits): the raise under an h=32 secret lets EvalMod use the
K=16 sparse coefficient set, and it exits with more towers (42 vs 34).
